  "preamble": "Devoicing and depalatalization of the voiced postalveolar affricate [dʒ] to [ts]. In Chamorro, Proto-Malayo-Polynesian *z (Blust: /dʒ/; some treatments /ɟ͡ʝ/) regularly became the affricate written ⟨ch⟩, usually pronounced [ts] (with [tʃ] before non-low front vowels); neighbouring Palauan instead turned *z into a flap r. In Cantonese, the Middle Chinese 崇 initial (Baxter dzr-), reconstructed by Zhengzhang Shangfang as postalveolar [dʒ], gives unaspirated z [ts] in oblique-tone words, merging with the dental 精 series; Karlgren and Pulleyblank reconstruct this initial as retroflex [ɖʐ] instead.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Devoicing, Depalatalization",
